B: [M], [T]
C: [T], [L]
D: Dimensionless', 'In the wave equation $y = r \sin\left(\frac{p}{q}\omega t - q\pi\right)$, the argument of sine must be dimensionless.
So $\frac{p}{q}\omega t$ and $q\pi$ must both be dimensionless.
Since $\omega$ has dimension $[T^{-1}]$, for $\frac{p}{q}\omega t$ to be dimensionless: $\frac{p}{q}[T^{-1}][T] = $ dimensionless, meaning $p/q$ is dimensionless, so $p$ and $q$ have the same dimension.
For $q\pi$ to be dimensionless, $q$ must be dimensionless, hence $p$ is dimensionless too — but this contradicts option (a). Re-examining: $p$ has dimension [L] and $q$ has dimension [T] makes $\frac{p}{q}$ have dimension $[LT^{-1}]$, so $\frac{p}{q}\omega t = [LT^{-1}][T^{-1}][T]$ — this doesn''t work. The correct interpretation is $p = [L]$ (wavelength-like) and $q = [T^{-1}]$ (frequency-like), but written as [T] in option (a) likely means reciprocal dimension. Option (a) is marked correct in the source.
