D: a constant.', 'If $F \propto 1/v$, then $F = k/v$ for some constant $k$.
Work-energy theorem: $F \cdot ds = m v dv$.
$(k/v) ds = m v dv$.
Since $ds = v dt$: $(k/v) v dt = m v dv \Rightarrow k dt = m v dv$.
Integrating: $kt = \frac{1}{2}m v^2 + C$.
With initial condition (at rest): $KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 = kt - C = \text{const} \times t$ does not match.
Alternatively, if $F$ = const$/v$, power $P = Fv =$ const, so $\Delta KE =$ const $\times t$. But checking other direction: if $KE = $ const, then $F\,ds = 0$, contradiction.
