B: $2\sqrt{5}\ \text{m s}^{-1}$
C: $2\sqrt{3}\ \text{m s}^{-1}$
D: $3\sqrt{2}\ \text{m s}^{-1}$', 'Height difference between maximum and lowest points: $\Delta h = 2.5 - 1.5 = 1\ \text{m}$.
At maximum height, velocity is zero; at lowest point, all potential energy converts to kinetic energy.
Using $v = \sqrt{2g\Delta h} = \sqrt{2 \times 10 \times 1} = \sqrt{20} = 2\sqrt{5}\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.
Wait, that matches option (b). Re-checking: $\sqrt{20} = \sqrt{4 \times 5} = 2\sqrt{5}$. But the answer key says (a), which is $5\sqrt{2} \approx 7.07$. Let me verify: $5\sqrt{2} = \sqrt{50}$, giving $\Delta h = \frac{50}{20} = 2.5\ \text{m}$, but our $\Delta h = 1\ \text{m}$. There may be a transcription issue in the original problem or answer key.
