Glucose: M = 0.05 M with molar mass 180, so molarity is 0.05 mol/L.
For unknown solute: 6 g with molarity 0.05 M gives molar mass = $\frac{6}{0.05} = 120$ g/mol.
Empirical formula mass (CH₂O) = 30. n = $\frac{120}{30} = 4$.
Molecular formula = (CH₂O)₄ = C₄H₈O₄.
Wait, checking: C₅H₁₀O₅ has molar mass 150, not 120.
Let me recalculate: If π is same, then n(solute)/V(solution) is same. Assuming V ≈ 1 L for both, molar mass of solute = 120.
(CH₂O)₄ = C₄H₈O₄ (120). But option shows C₅H₁₀O₅ (150).
Re-examining: if 6g gives same osmotic pressure, molar mass = 120. But C₄H₈O₄ is not offered correctly or calculation differs.
