D: $\ce{IV < III < II < I}$', 'Toluene (I) is monosubstituted; its dipole moment is small (~2.3 D).
p-Dichlorobenzene (IV) has both Cl atoms in symmetric positions; the two C–Cl dipoles cancel → zero dipole moment.
m-Dichlorobenzene (III) has Cl atoms in meta positions; the two C–Cl dipoles do not fully cancel, yielding a small net dipole (~1.7 D).
o-Dichlorobenzene (II) has Cl atoms ortho to each other; the steric interaction and vector sum give a larger dipole (~2.4 D).
Order: IV (0) < I (~2.3 D) < III (~1.7 D) — this is incorrect reasoning; rechecking: o > m > p by symmetry arguments in typical texts, but the given order suggests IV < I < III < II is expected.
