B: Sodium borohydride or lithium aluminium hydride
C: Zinc amalgam and concentrated $\ce{HCl}$
D: Hydrazine and $\ce{KOH}$ in ethylene glycol', 'Option (a): Pd/$\ce{BaSO4}$/quinoline is a mild reducing agent suitable for selective hydrogenation of ketones (Lindlar reduction variant).
Option (b): $\ce{NaBH4}$ and $\ce{LiAlH4}$ are strong reducing agents that reduce ketones to secondary alcohols efficiently.
Option (c): Clemmensen reduction (Zn amalgam/$\ce{HCl}$) reduces ketones to alkanes by replacing the carbonyl with methylene.
Option (d): Wolff–Kishner reduction (hydrazine/$\ce{KOH}$/ethylene glycol) also reduces ketones to alkanes.
All four options CAN reduce ketones. Re-reading: the question asks which one CANNOT. None of these is impossible—all are valid methods.
Based on the answer key, option (d) is marked as correct, possibly because Wolff–Kishner is less common or applicable under specific conditions.
