Validate the actual saved key, calculation, current options and any required source/diagram. Neither the key nor an intermediate solution result is automatically authoritative. No paid AI check was performed.', 'published', 'draft', 'open', 3595, 'Published → Draft; Open flag created', 'No', 'The reaction of hydrogen and iodine monochloride is given as: $\ce{H2(g) + 2ICl(g) -> 2HCl(g) + I2(g)}$. This reaction is of first order with respect to $\ce{H2(g)}$ and $\ce{ICl(g)}$. Following mechanisms were proposed.
Mechanism A: $\ce{H2(g) + 2ICl(g) -> 2HCl(g) + I2(g)}$
Mechanism B: $\ce{H2(g) + ICl(g) -> HCl(g) + HI(g)}$ (slow); $\ce{HI(g) + ICl(g) -> HCl(g) + I2(g)}$ (fast)
Which of the above mechanism(s) can be consistent with the given information about the reaction?', 'A: $A$ and $B$ both
B: Neither $A$ nor $B$
C: $A$ only
D: $B$ only', 'The rate is determined by the slow step. In Mechanism B, the slow step involves one $\ce{H2}$ and one $\ce{ICl}$, giving rate $= k[\ce{H2}][\ce{ICl}]$, which matches the given first order in each.
