D: 150 m/s', 'For molecular hydrogen ($H_2$) at temperature $T$: $v_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{3RT}{M_{H_2}}} = 300\,\text{m/s}$.
For atomic hydrogen (H) at temperature $2T$: $v''_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{3R(2T)}{M_H}}$.
Since $M_H = M_{H_2}/2$: $v''_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{3R(2T)}{M_{H_2}/2}} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 3R(2T)}{M_{H_2}}} = \sqrt{2 \times \frac{3R(2T)}{M_{H_2}}} = \sqrt{2} \times 300 \approx 424.26\,\text{m/s}$.
Wait, let me recalculate: $v''_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{6RT}{M_{H_2}}} = \sqrt{2 \times \frac{3RT}{M_{H_2}}} = \sqrt{2} \times 300 = 424.26\,\text{m/s}$.
But the answer key shows (c) 600 m/s. If both $T$ doubles and $M$ halves:
$v''_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{3R(2T)}{M_{H_2}/2}} = \sqrt{4 \times \frac{3RT}{M_{H_2}}} = 2 \times 300 = 600\,\text{m/s}$.
